--TEST--
Core\Validate::isEmail refuses a bracketed IP address as the mailbox host and isDomain refuses a name written with its root dot, where filter_var accepts both
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
Both of PHP's answers here are format corners rather than what the caller asked about — `FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL` accepts RFC 5321's `local@[127.0.0.1]` form, an IP address in brackets as the host, so a value that passed a mailbox check can carry an IP into code that assumed a hostname, and `FILTER_VALIDATE_DOMAIN` under `FILTER_FLAG_HOSTNAME` accepts the fully-qualified `example.com.`, whose trailing dot compares unequal to every allowlist entry that omits it; spec § 12 names each member after a *format* and neither of these is the format a program that calls `isEmail` or `isDomain` is naming.
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The agreement over the rest of both formats is
// `validate-answers-what-filter_vars-validate-half-answers`; this case is only
// the two subjects where the two languages part, and each is a value that
// passes PHP's check and then means something else downstream.
final class Show {
    public static function of(bool $answer): string {
        return $answer ? "1" : "0";
    }
}

// `filter_var("user@[127.0.0.1]", FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL)` answers the string,
// and the IPv6 spelling with it. A program that then treats the host half as a
// name — an allowlist, an MX lookup, a log line — is holding an address it did
// not ask for, which is why `isEmail` names the format it names.
array<string> $bracketed = [
    "user@[127.0.0.1]",
    "user@[IPv6:2001:db8::1]",
];
foreach ($bracketed as string $subject) {
    echo Show::of(Core\Validate::isEmail($subject));
}
echo "\n";

// The ordinary spelling of the same mailbox is accepted on both sides, so the
// refusal above is about the brackets and not about the host being numeric.
echo Show::of(Core\Validate::isEmail("user@example.com")), "\n";

// `filter_var("example.com.", FILTER_VALIDATE_DOMAIN, FILTER_FLAG_HOSTNAME)`
// answers the string including the trailing dot, so the value that passed is
// not the value an allowlist holds. A bare root dot and a doubled one are
// refused on both sides, which bounds the divergence to the one form.
array<string> $names = [
    "example.com.",
    "example.com..",
    ".",
];
foreach ($names as string $subject) {
    echo Show::of(Core\Validate::isDomain($subject));
}
echo "\n";

// And the same name without it is accepted on both sides.
echo Show::of(Core\Validate::isDomain("example.com")), "\n";
--EXPECT--
00
1
000
1
